WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.695
Hi, I'm Coracle!

00:00:02.695 --> 00:00:05.976
Ssftalk says it is an ElevenLabs engine in pure Rust.

00:00:05.976 --> 00:00:08.602
Here is how it actually makes a voice.

00:00:10.308 --> 00:00:12.475
There is no neural network here.

00:00:12.475 --> 00:00:17.532
A voice is a pitch and four resonances shaping a buzz, behind ElevenLabs-shaped requests.

00:00:19.258 --> 00:00:21.906
A request arrives in the ElevenLabs format.

00:00:21.906 --> 00:00:24.933
Hand-written letter rules turn the words into phonemes.

00:00:25.353 --> 00:00:29.615
The voice is a preset: one pitch, four formants, a tilt and some breath.

00:00:29.615 --> 00:00:31.137
Even Rachel is six numbers.

00:00:31.557 --> 00:00:34.053
A synthesizer shapes a buzz with those resonances.

00:00:34.053 --> 00:00:37.173
Its source ships encrypted, so I could not read it.

00:00:38.483 --> 00:00:40.543
Four modules are not in readable form.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:43.575
The build script reads one encrypted file.

00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:46.941
It undoes a simple cipher whose key sits in the script.

00:00:47.361 --> 00:00:51.310
It writes out the synthesizer, recognizer, isolation and room code.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:52.980
Then compiles them in.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.792
Ten of the fourteen benchmark stages run that code.

00:00:57.233 --> 00:00:58.357
Here is the formula.

00:00:59.233 --> 00:01:02.401
Each benchmark stage times a loop of ssftalk calls.

00:01:02.821 --> 00:01:05.150
Then divides a number typed into the file.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.187
None of the rival tools ever runs.

00:01:09.108 --> 00:01:12.307
The audio language model saving is an amplitude gate.

00:01:12.307 --> 00:01:13.728
It marks loud samples.

00:01:14.148 --> 00:01:18.305
It counts them per frame, but an integer division shifts every frame's window.

00:01:18.725 --> 00:01:23.291
On a test second that is ninety percent silence, that gives the eighty-eight percent.

00:01:23.291 --> 00:01:24.921
Real speech gives far less.

00:01:26.758 --> 00:01:27.840
Their benchmark table.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.887
The baseline column is typed constants, so the headline compares the crate with nothing.

00:01:34.683 --> 00:01:38.237
A speedup is only real when both sides run on the same

00:01:38.237 --> 00:01:40.902
input and machine, with quality reported next to time.

00:01:43.483 --> 00:01:44.336
Six numbers.

00:01:44.336 --> 00:01:45.616
An encrypted synthesizer.

00:01:45.616 --> 00:01:47.749
Baselines typed in, not run.

00:01:48.169 --> 00:01:50.305
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:50.305 --> 00:01:50.915
I'm Coracle.

00:01:50.915 --> 00:01:51.220
Bye!
